<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludwig Erhard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1977">1977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludwig Erhard was a German economist and politician of the Christian Democratic Union who, as director of economics in the western zones, ended price controls alongside the currency reform of 1948. As economics minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> from 1949 to 1963 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-adenauer">Konrad Adenauer</a> he promoted the social market economy and is credited with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wirtschaftswunder">Wirtschaftswunder</a>. He succeeded Adenauer as chancellor from 1963 to 1966.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludwig-erhard.jpg" alt="Ludwig Erhard" width="320" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Koch for Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Einde_bezoek_bondskanselier_dr_Ludwig_Erhard_en_gaf_persconferentie_in_het_Haag,_Bestanddeelnr_916-1330.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ludwig Erhard was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/furth">Fürth</a>, in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, on 4 February 1897, the son of a Catholic clothing store owner. Polio in early childhood left him with a deformed right foot. He trained as a textile apprentice and worked in his father's draper's shop until he volunteered for the army in 1916. A shell wounded him badly on 28 September 1918, and seven operations left his left arm permanently shorter than his right.</p><p>Unable to return to draper's work, he studied economics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> and then at the Goethe University Frankfurt, where he took his doctorate in 1925 under Franz Oppenheimer, whose opposition to monopolies influenced him. From 1928 he worked at a market research institute in Nuremberg, and he lost that post in 1942 after working on plans for a postwar peace that the Nazis had forbidden.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1944 Erhard wrote a study of war finance and debt consolidation that assumed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had already lost the war. After the war he led the expert commission on money and credit that prepared the currency reform of the western zones, and in April 1948 the Bizonal Economic Council elected him director of economics. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutsche-mark">Deutsche Mark</a> was introduced on 20 June 1948, and Erhard abolished the price-fixing and production controls of the military administration, a step beyond his authority.</p><p>Elected to the Bundestag in 1949 for the Christian Democratic Union, he served as Federal Minister for Economic Affairs for fourteen years and as vice-chancellor from 1957 to 1963. An economic liberal, he joined the Mont Pelerin Society in 1950. In 1957 he suffered defeats, notably over anti-cartel legislation, and the West German economy then developed into a conventional welfare state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Parliament elected Erhard chancellor on 16 October 1963 by 279 votes to 180. His government introduced housing benefit in 1965 and established diplomatic relations with Israel that year. His support for the American role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a> strained his coalition, and during the recession of 1966 the ministers of the Free Democratic Party resigned over the budget. He resigned on 30 November 1966, and Kurt Georg Kiesinger succeeded him. He remained in the Bundestag and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> on 5 May 1977.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ludwig Erhard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>There the economists around <b>Ludwig Erhard</b> drew on theories developed in the 1930s and 1940s, and Alfred Müller-Armack coined the phrase “social market economy”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/neoliberalism">Neoliberalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/furth">Fürth</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.48,10.99&amp;year=1949" aria-label="Fürth on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4803 440 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ludwig Erhard"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5253 710h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5253 710h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.48,10.99&amp;year=1949">What was here in 1949? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
